        <h2 id="why-china">Why China Still Leads Global Jacket Manufacturing</h2>
        <p>China's edge in jacket production is not cheap labor. It is <strong>vertical integration</strong>. Within a two-hour drive of most major jacket clusters, you can source 75D shell fabric, taffeta lining, YKK or local-equivalent zippers, drawcords, woven labels, and polybags, then drop the cut-and-sew order into a factory that already runs the same construction every week. That density compresses lead times and strips out the coordination risk that drags down multi-country sourcing</p>
        <p>According to WTO trade statistics, China has ranked first in global apparel exports by value for years, and outerwear sits among its strongest categories. For a jacket buyer, the practical meaning is depth of capability. Quilting, seam sealing, down filling, garment washing, and 12-head embroidery all exist as mature, competitive sub-industries rather than favors a factory does on the side. Cluster density raises the average quality of the supplier pool, but it never guarantees that one specific factory fits your MOQ, your compliance requirements, or your ship window</p>
        
        <h2 id="method">How We Selected These 10 Manufacturer Types (Our Methodology)</h2>
        <p>The YAUUM Sourcing Team scored candidates against six dimensions that actually change how an order lands:</p>
        <ol class="ar-list">
        <li><strong>Category specialization</strong>. Weekly production of your jacket type, not seasonal</li>
        <li><strong>MOQ flexibility</strong>. Room for a 300-piece test order</li>
        <li><strong>Compliance and certification</strong>. Social audits and testing your market demands</li>
        <li><strong>Sampling capability</strong>. In-house patterns, fit samples, lab dips</li>
        <li><strong>Export experience</strong>. Labeling, carton marking, customs paperwork fluency</li>
        <li><strong>Communication responsiveness</strong>. English account management, timelines that hold</li>
        </ol>
        <p>We deliberately did <strong>not</strong> rank by factory size. A 2,000-worker plant is the wrong home for a startup's first 200-unit run, and a 40-machine workshop will choke on a 50,000-unit seasonal program. Each entry below states plainly who it suits and who should walk away</p>
        <p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> YAUUM operates in apparel manufacturing and sourcing. Some supplier types described here overlap with partners and competitors. The selection criteria are written so you can apply them independently, and every "what to verify" note can be checked without us. For background on what factory certifications actually cover, see our guide to <a href="/our-services/">social compliance certifications for apparel factories</a></p>
        
